<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<bpmn:definitions xmlns:bpmn="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL"
                  xmlns:bpmndi="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/DI"
                  xmlns:dc="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DC"
                  xmlns:di="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DI"
                  xmlns:folio="https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/bpmn"
                  id="Definitions_CriterionAdjudication"
                  targetNamespace="https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/workflows">

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Criterion adjudication — the QA-criterion SPECIALISATION
       Bean: folio-assistant-bvuk · issue #1073

       WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, stated so nobody merges it back.

       `adjudication.bpmn` used to hold both halves: the judgement, and
       the three things done about it. Six diagrams called it, and only
       two — `review-narrative` and `voice-review` — asked a question
       those three answer. `refresh-materialized` asks which side wins a
       local-vs-remote conflict; `ingest-l1-completeness-gate` asks
       whether a drift is real. Neither has a criterion to scope or a
       dispensation to grant, and a callActivity runs the whole
       subprocess, so both ran the outcome half regardless.

       The first proposed fix was to hand each caller its own codes. It
       does not work: the codes selected three different TASKS, so
       `local-wins` would have pointed at A_ScopeCriterion. The branches
       had to move, not the enum.

       So the split is AT THE JUDGEMENT. Everything that must not vary
       stays in Process_Adjudication — the entry condition, the untainted
       dispatch, the person-or-agent restriction. Everything that depends
       on WHAT WAS ASKED lives with the asker, and this is the asker for
       the QA-criterion question.

       Skill: skills/folio-core/adjudication.md
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <bpmn:collaboration id="Collab_CriterionAdjudication">
    <bpmn:participant id="Participant_CriterionAdjudication" name="Criterion adjudication" processRef="Process_CriterionAdjudication"/>
  </bpmn:collaboration>

  <bpmn:process id="Process_CriterionAdjudication" name="Criterion adjudication" isExecutable="true">
    <bpmn:documentation>Two reviewer entries for ONE QA criterion disagree: ask the adjudication, then do the one of three things that a criterion disagreement admits.

You are in this process when the disagreement is about a criterion — a script failing what a reviewer passed, an agent and a person reading the same block differently. If what disagrees is not a criterion, this is the wrong process and `adjudication.bpmn` is the one to call directly, with your own outcomes.

The three outcomes are legitimate in the same way, and it is not which one is chosen: the finding stands, the criterion does not apply here and should be scoped, or it applies and this is a stated exception. All three converge on A_RecordEntry, because what makes an outcome sound is that its reason was recorded.

ADVISORY at the process level, for the reason the adjudication itself is: a judgement cannot be gated, since the whole reason it was entered is that the mechanism ran out of facts. What is NOT relaxable is A_RecordEntry. A judgement nobody wrote down is indistinguishable from a checker that was never run, so the judgement is free and the record is not.</bpmn:documentation>
    <bpmn:extensionElements>
      <folio:policy enforcement="advisory"/>
    </bpmn:extensionElements>

    <bpmn:laneSet id="LaneSet_CriterionAdjudication">
      <bpmn:lane id="Lane_CriterionAdjudicator" name="Adjudicator">
        <bpmn:documentation>One lane, because every step here is the adjudicator's: asking the question, choosing among the answers, and writing the entry. The feedback provider's lane is in `adjudication.bpmn`, where stating a finding happens — a provider has no entitlement to settle what it reported, and drawing it here would suggest otherwise.

The role admits person and agent. `refresh-materialized` already gives the reason in its own words — "a human or agentic decision, never a merge rule" — and the steps are plain tasks rather than user tasks so the diagram does not assert a person where an agent is legitimate.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:role ref="adjudicator"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Start_CriterionDisagreement</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Call_Adjudicate</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GW_Outcome</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_ScopeCriterion</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_Dispensation</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_RecordEntry</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_Settled</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
    </bpmn:laneSet>

    <bpmn:startEvent id="Start_CriterionDisagreement" name="Entries for one criterion disagree">
      <bpmn:documentation>The sidecar, not a complaint. `block-qa/v1` allows several entries per criterion and declares three reviewer kinds, so "they disagree" is read off the record rather than asserted by whoever is unhappy.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_C1</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:startEvent>

    <bpmn:callActivity id="Call_Adjudicate" name="Adjudicate the criterion disagreement" calledElement="Process_Adjudication">
      <bpmn:documentation>The shared judgement — entry condition, untainted dispatch, person-or-agent restriction — asked with THIS question's permitted answers. `folio:adjudication codes` sits here rather than on `A_Adjudicate` inside it because the answers depend on what was asked, and the gateway below is what acts on them: the engine refuses a mismatch between the two.

`Process_Adjudication` may also leave at `End_NotAdjudicable` — one entry and no other side is a review or a gate, not a disagreement — in which case nothing below runs and there is nothing to record.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements>
        <folio:skill ref="adjudication"/>
        <folio:adjudication codes="stands scope dispensation" list="adjudication-criterion"/>
        <folio:fulfilment kinds="person agent" reason="the same restriction the called process states on A_Adjudicate, restated here because this is where the enum is declared and a step naming permitted answers without saying who may choose among them has restricted nobody. It cannot drift wrong: the engine refuses `system` or `external` on any step carrying `folio:adjudication`, so the only failure available is absence, and absence is refused too."/>
      </bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_C1</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_C2</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:callActivity>

    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GW_Outcome" name="Which of the three?">
      <bpmn:extensionElements>
        <folio:judgement reason="This is THE judgement — the branch the whole process exists to take, and the one thing nothing here can compute. A table deciding it would decide the case that, by the entry condition, no mechanism could: the checker and the reviewer both read the artefact and reached different answers, so a rule keying on either would be picking a side by construction rather than by reason."/>
      </bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:documentation>The three are legitimate in the same way: what makes an outcome sound is not which one it is but that its reason was recorded. All three converge on A_RecordEntry for exactly that reason.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_C2</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_Stands</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_Scope</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_Exception</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>

    <bpmn:task id="A_ScopeCriterion" name="Scope the criterion so it stops applying here">
      <bpmn:documentation>One edit to the criterion rather than ten overrules on the blocks it should never have covered. This branch is the one an adjudicator under time pressure converts into a dispensation, and the conversion is a defect: a dispensation lapses when the source moves, so a mis-scoped criterion granted its way past comes back every time anybody touches the block.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="adjudication"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_Scope</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_C5</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:task id="A_Dispensation" name="Grant a dispensation, with its reason">
      <bpmn:documentation>The rule applies and this subject is a stated exception. A `kind: "human"` entry with `result: "pass"` overrides the script's `fail` for that criterion, and ONLY while its `field_hash` matches the current source — so the dispensation is scoped to the version it was granted against, lapses when the block changes, and never becomes precedent. There is nothing to overturn later; the source moving overturns it.

A dispensation with no `notes` is not a dispensation. It is an override somebody applied to get to green, and nobody can review it afterwards — which is why this step is not relaxable.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements>
        <folio:skill ref="adjudication"/>
        <folio:policy relaxable="false"/>
      </bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_Exception</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_C6</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:task id="A_RecordEntry" name="Write the entry that LEADS — keeping the checker's beneath it">
      <bpmn:documentation>Not relaxable, and the one step here that is not. A judgement nobody wrote down is indistinguishable from a checker that was never run, so the judgement is free and the record is not.

Do not resolve the disagreement away: both entries stay, the adjudication on top under the sweep's most-recent-matching-hash rule. A disagreement between a checker and a reviewer is information, and somebody re-running the check next month needs to find that a checker read it differently rather than a clean pass. `/api/relevance/adjudicate` reached the same rule independently — `human_adjudicated` written alongside, never over, the agent's `assessed_by`.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements>
        <folio:skill ref="adjudication"/>
        <folio:policy relaxable="false"/>
      </bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_Stands</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_C5</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_C6</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_C7</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:endEvent id="End_Settled" name="Settled, with the reason on the record">
      <bpmn:documentation>Settled for this version of the source. The sidecar now holds both readings and the reason one of them leads.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_C7</bpmn:incoming>
    </bpmn:endEvent>

    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_C1" sourceRef="Start_CriterionDisagreement" targetRef="Call_Adjudicate"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_C2" sourceRef="Call_Adjudicate" targetRef="GW_Outcome"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_Stands" name="the finding stands" sourceRef="GW_Outcome" targetRef="A_RecordEntry">
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:adjudication code="stands"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:sequenceFlow>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_Scope" name="the criterion does not apply here" sourceRef="GW_Outcome" targetRef="A_ScopeCriterion">
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:adjudication code="scope"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:sequenceFlow>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_Exception" name="it applies; this is an exception" sourceRef="GW_Outcome" targetRef="A_Dispensation">
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:adjudication code="dispensation"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:sequenceFlow>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_C5" sourceRef="A_ScopeCriterion" targetRef="A_RecordEntry"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_C6" sourceRef="A_Dispensation" targetRef="A_RecordEntry"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_C7" sourceRef="A_RecordEntry" targetRef="End_Settled"/>
  </bpmn:process>

  <!-- Layout only. Nothing below changes what the process does. -->
  <bpmndi:BPMNDiagram id="BPMNDiagram_CriterionAdjudication">
    <bpmndi:BPMNPlane id="BPMNPlane_CriterionAdjudication" bpmnElement="Collab_CriterionAdjudication">
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_Participant_CriterionAdjudication" bpmnElement="Participant_CriterionAdjudication" isHorizontal="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="160" y="60" width="1180" height="420"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_Lane_CriterionAdjudicator" bpmnElement="Lane_CriterionAdjudicator" isHorizontal="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="190" y="60" width="1150" height="420"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>

      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_Start_CriterionDisagreement" bpmnElement="Start_CriterionDisagreement">
        <dc:Bounds x="230" y="132" width="36" height="36"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_Call_Adjudicate" bpmnElement="Call_Adjudicate">
        <dc:Bounds x="320" y="110" width="170" height="80"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_GW_Outcome" bpmnElement="GW_Outcome" isMarkerVisible="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="550" y="125" width="50" height="50"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_A_ScopeCriterion" bpmnElement="A_ScopeCriterion">
        <dc:Bounds x="650" y="230" width="150" height="80"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_A_Dispensation" bpmnElement="A_Dispensation">
        <dc:Bounds x="650" y="350" width="150" height="80"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_A_RecordEntry" bpmnElement="A_RecordEntry">
        <dc:Bounds x="880" y="110" width="160" height="80"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="Shape_End_Settled" bpmnElement="End_Settled">
        <dc:Bounds x="1110" y="132" width="36" height="36"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>

      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_F_C1" bpmnElement="F_C1">
        <di:waypoint x="266" y="150"/>
        <di:waypoint x="320" y="150"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_F_C2" bpmnElement="F_C2">
        <di:waypoint x="490" y="150"/>
        <di:waypoint x="550" y="150"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_F_Stands" bpmnElement="F_Stands">
        <di:waypoint x="600" y="150"/>
        <di:waypoint x="880" y="150"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_F_Scope" bpmnElement="F_Scope">
        <di:waypoint x="575" y="175"/>
        <di:waypoint x="575" y="270"/>
        <di:waypoint x="650" y="270"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_F_Exception" bpmnElement="F_Exception">
        <di:waypoint x="575" y="175"/>
        <di:waypoint x="575" y="390"/>
        <di:waypoint x="650" y="390"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_F_C5" bpmnElement="F_C5">
        <di:waypoint x="800" y="270"/>
        <di:waypoint x="960" y="270"/>
        <di:waypoint x="960" y="190"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_F_C6" bpmnElement="F_C6">
        <di:waypoint x="800" y="390"/>
        <di:waypoint x="960" y="390"/>
        <di:waypoint x="960" y="190"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="Edge_F_C7" bpmnElement="F_C7">
        <di:waypoint x="1040" y="150"/>
        <di:waypoint x="1110" y="150"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
    </bpmndi:BPMNPlane>
  </bpmndi:BPMNDiagram>
</bpmn:definitions>
